From: Stained Glass OS <dev@stained-glass.example>
Subject: dwmapi: DwmFlush waits for the next vertical blank

DwmFlush returns once the next composition pass is done, i.e. after the
next vertical blank. Wine's returned at once (a stub).

Firefox's vsync thread (D3DVsyncSource::VBlankLoop) is `notify; DwmFlush()`
in a loop, so it notified thousands of times a second. With the GPU
process each notification is an IPC message to it: the GPU process's I/O
thread never caught up, synchronous requests to it timed out ("Killing GPU
process due to IPC reply timeout"), the browser's queued messages grew to
tens of GB, pages did not load and closing the window left the processes
running. Without the GPU process the same loop kept the main thread busy
and shutdown took minutes. Debian's stock Wine behaves the same.

It now sleeps until the next refresh period boundary -- the same vblank
grid DwmGetCompositionTimingInfo already reports (qpcVBlank is a multiple
of qpcRefreshPeriod) -- so the two agree.

Gates: test/vsync-gate.sh (DwmFlush's pace, and Firefox's pattern: vsync
over an overlapped named pipe, an I/O completion port and a posted-message
wake), test/firefox-e2e.sh (the pinned Firefox shows a local page with its
GPU process and every process exits after the window closes).
---
--- a/dlls/dwmapi/dwmapi_main.c
+++ b/dlls/dwmapi/dwmapi_main.c
@@ -33,6 +33,8 @@
 
 WINE_DEFAULT_DEBUG_CHANNEL(dwmapi);
 
+static int get_display_frequency(void);
+
 
 /**********************************************************************
  *           DwmIsCompositionEnabled         (DWMAPI.@)
@@ -89,10 +91,22 @@
  */
 HRESULT WINAPI DwmFlush(void)
 {
-    static BOOL once;
+    LARGE_INTEGER frequency, qpc, delay;
+    LONGLONG period, next;
 
-    if (!once++) FIXME("() stub\n");
+    TRACE("()\n");
 
+    /* DwmFlush returns after the next composition pass, which happens once per
+     * vertical blank.  Returning at once turns every caller that paces itself
+     * with it (such as Firefox's vsync thread) into a busy loop. */
+    QueryPerformanceFrequency( &frequency );
+    period = frequency.QuadPart / get_display_frequency();
+    if (period <= 0) return S_OK;
+    QueryPerformanceCounter( &qpc );
+    next = (qpc.QuadPart / period + 1) * period;
+    delay.QuadPart = -((next - qpc.QuadPart) * 10000000 / frequency.QuadPart);
+    if (!delay.QuadPart) delay.QuadPart = -1;
+    NtDelayExecution( FALSE, &delay );
     return S_OK;
 }
 
